---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/ui/SectionHead.astro';

const askPrimitive = `ask(question: string) -> string`;

const contents = [
  { num: '01', href: '#messaging', label: 'The Big Idea Was Messaging' },
  { num: '02', href: '#compression', label: 'Compression, Not Abstraction' },
  { num: '03', href: '#metasystem', label: 'The Ultimate Metasystem' },
  { num: '04', href: '#theory-artifact', label: 'Theory and Artifact' },
  { num: '05', href: '#dci', label: 'Data, Context, and Interaction' },
  { num: '06', href: '#love-letter', label: 'A Love Letter' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="The Theory - Abject"
  description="The ideas behind Abject: Alan Kay's big idea was messaging, compression versus abstraction, Naur's programs as theory, Alexander's pattern languages, and DCI."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="theory">

    <!-- Hero -->
    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Theory</span></p>
        <h1 class="page-hero-title band">The Theory</h1>
        <p class="page-hero-lede">
          When I say the internet is an object-oriented system, people trained in programming
          have a hard time with it, because we were taught that object-oriented means classes and
          class hierarchies. That was never the big idea. Alan Kay said the big idea was
          messaging: little computers that hold their own state and send each other messages.
          That idea runs your cells and it runs the internet. Abject takes the original idea
          seriously and adds one thing that wasn't possible in 1972: objects that can explain
          themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container theory-grid">

      <!-- Side rail: the longform pointer and the contents -->
      <aside class="theory-rail">
        <div class="rail-block rail-note">
          <p class="rail-label">Longform</p>
          <p>
            <strong>This is the short version.</strong> The long one is my post
            <a href="https://blog.mempko.com/a-love-letter-to-object-orientation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A&nbsp;Love&nbsp;Letter&nbsp;to&nbsp;Object&nbsp;Orientation</a>.
            For how it works rather than why, read <a href="/ask-protocol">the Ask Protocol specification</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <nav class="rail-block toc" aria-labelledby="toc-title">
          <h2 class="rail-label" id="toc-title">Contents</h2>
          <ol class="toc-list">
            {contents.map((c) => (
              <li>
                <a href={c.href}>
                  <span class="toc-num">{c.num}</span>
                  <span class="toc-label">{c.label}</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </nav>
      </aside>

      <article class="theory-body">

        <!-- 01 -->
        <section id="messaging" class="th-sec">
          <SectionHead num="01" title="The Big Idea Was Messaging" />

          <p>
            Alan Kay popularized the ideas behind object-oriented programming, and later said he
            was sorry he coined the term "objects" for them, because it got people to focus on the
            lesser idea. In 1998 he wrote to the Squeak mailing list:
          </p>

          <blockquote class="quote">
            <p>The big idea is "messaging".</p>
            <p>
              The key in making great and growable systems is much more to design how its modules
              communicate rather than what their internal properties and behaviors should be.
            </p>
            <cite>Alan Kay, 1998</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            In the same message he mentions <em>ma</em>, a small Japanese word for "that which is
            in between." What matters in a system is less what's inside each part and more what
            happens between the parts: which messages cross, in what form, and on whose terms.
          </p>

          <p>
            The internet is the proof. It isn't one giant class hierarchy. It's independent systems
            sending messages across agreed boundaries, an estimated 30 billion devices of them.
            Since the first message went over ARPANET in 1969, all the atoms of the system have been
            replaced, and it has never shut down. Biology got there first. Kay was inspired by
            cells, which coordinate by sending chemical messages to each other. A white blood cell
            meeting a pathogen doesn't look up an API. It reads the chemical signals on the surface
            and responds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meanwhile the languages that took the name went another way. They built classes and
            class hierarchies, all about what's inside an object and almost nothing about what
            passes between objects. We kept the word and lost the idea.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 02 -->
        <section id="compression" class="th-sec">
          <SectionHead num="02" title="Compression, Not Abstraction" />

          <p>
            There is a big problem with object systems, though. The messages between objects have
            to be coded by people. If two objects speak different message formats, someone has to
            write the bridge. Every integration, adapter, and SDK is a person reading one side's
            documentation and translating it for the other side. That's most of what software work
            has been, and when one interface changes, anything talking to it breaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            People have tried to fix this before. CORBA in the 1990s, SOAP and WSDL in the 2000s,
            and protocols like UPnP all tried to make services describe themselves. They all
            collapsed under the weight of rigid schemas, verbose XML contracts, and version
            incompatibilities. Abject adds one message that every object handles:
          </p>

          <pre class="primitive"><code>{askPrimitive}</code></pre>

          <p>
            That's the Ask Protocol. It's that simple. You ask any object a question in plain
            English and it answers. Natural language works where schemas failed because it is
            flexible, and an LLM can handle ambiguity, paraphrase, and partial information. What
            makes the answer more than a documentation string is where it comes from.
          </p>

          <h3>Abstraction throws the code away</h3>

          <p>
            An abstraction throws the implementation away. Somebody decided once what mattered,
            wrote that down, and discarded the rest. An interface definition, a manifest, and a
            schema all work this way. Because it was frozen at one moment, it goes stale. When the
            implementation changes, the abstraction either leaks or quietly lies. Anyone working
            from manifests alone is working from someone else's frozen, lossy judgment about what
            mattered, made at some earlier date, for some other purpose.
          </p>

          <h3>Compression reads the code every time</h3>

          <p>
            The ask handler doesn't do that. It runs inside the object, with access to the actual
            code. When it answers a question it isn't reciting a summary somebody wrote once. It's
            deriving one, right now, from the real thing. That's compression, not abstraction. It's
            still lossy, since any answer is shorter than the program, but it's faithful to what the
            program does today, and it gets derived again every time somebody asks. Change the
            object and the next answer changes with it.
          </p>

          <aside class="field-paper note">
            <p class="note-label">Note</p>
            <p>
              <strong>This is why the manifest isn't enough.</strong> The manifest is the cheap
              answer, and objects fall back to it when no model is available. The useful answer is
              the one derived from the code that's running when you ask.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- 03 -->
        <section id="metasystem" class="th-sec">
          <SectionHead num="03" title="The Ultimate Metasystem" />

          <p>
            LLMs are good at writing code in the small, so they can build these bridges
            automatically. The Ask Protocol is what lets them do it, because the LLM can have a
            conversation with the objects to find out how they work. An email object with a dinner
            invitation asks a calendar object it has never met how to save it. The calendar answers
            with the message shape it accepts. The glue code gets generated and the event gets
            created. No SDK, no integration sprint, no meeting between two teams.
          </p>

          <p>
            The part that's easy to miss is that this is a conversation, not a lookup. When an
            object handles an ask, it knows who is asking, so it can ask back. What are you actually
            trying to do? Are those dollars or cents? What should happen when this fails? It can push
            back on a request, refuse one it thinks is a mistake, or send the caller to a better
            object. The Ask Protocol turns an ambiguous integration into a negotiated one.
            Documentation can't ask you a clarifying question.
          </p>

          <p>
            That's what I mean by the ultimate metasystem. Systems used to connect only as far as
            someone had written the connections by hand. When objects can teach each other, they
            can connect as far as they can explain themselves, and that grows on its own. You no
            longer have to glue them together manually.
          </p>

          <p class="aside">
            How it actually works (the message envelopes, the introspect interface, proxy
            generation, and self-healing) is in <a href="/ask-protocol">the Ask Protocol specification</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 04 -->
        <section id="theory-artifact" class="th-sec">
          <SectionHead num="04" title="Theory and Artifact" />

          <p>
            There's an idea I keep coming back to from Peter Naur's 1985 essay "Programming as
            Theory Building." The program is not the main product of a programmer's work. The main
            product is a <em>theory</em>: an understanding of the problem, why it has the shape it
            has, and why this structure solves it well. The code is an artifact produced from that
            theory, a record of decisions frozen at one moment. You can't recover the theory by
            reading the code. When the people who held the theory leave, the program is dead in a
            meaningful sense, even if it still compiles and runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Abject takes this literally. An object in Abject is not precious. Objects get
            rewritten, respawned, and garbage collected when a better version exists. What builds
            up instead is a knowledge base: facts, lessons learned from things that went wrong,
            insights, references, and patterns. It isn't documentation you write after the fact.
            It's a running record of everything the system has figured out that's worth carrying
            forward.
          </p>

          <h3>Patterns as a living vocabulary</h3>

          <p>
            This is Christopher Alexander's idea of a pattern language. It's not a checklist of
            solutions. It's a living vocabulary of tested resolutions, where each pattern names a
            recurring tension, the forces that make it hard, and the structural move that resolves
            them. When a goal finishes, Abject mines what happened for patterns. The ones that
            survive contact with real tasks get promoted, and from then on they're matched against
            new tasks and handed to the agent doing the work.
          </p>

          <p class="poster-quote th-quote">
            You could throw every object away, rebuild from the knowledge base and the pattern language, and get a system that works at least as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Probably better, because you'd be building from understanding rather than from habit.
            The objects are just the artifacts the theory happens to have produced so far.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 05 -->
        <section id="dci" class="th-sec">
          <SectionHead num="05" title="Data, Context, and Interaction" />

          <p>
            Data, Context, and Interaction (DCI) is an evolution of object-oriented thinking by
            Trygve Reenskaug, who gave us MVC, and James Coplien. Objects that hold stable domain
            data are easy to reason about, but real behavior doesn't live in any one of them. It
            lives in a scenario that several objects take part in. So DCI keeps the data in the
            objects, moves the scenario into a Context, and describes the roles objects play in it
            as the Interaction. It keeps messaging readable without turning every behavior into
            another class hierarchy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Abject extends that into a workflow. When you ask the chat to do something, Abject
            creates a goal, breaks it into tasks, gathers or creates the objects needed for those
            tasks, and sends them messages until the goal is done. The Ask Protocol lets each object
            explain how to work with it, so the workflow is composed instead of hard-coded.
          </p>

          <div class="dci">
            <div class="dci-col">
              <span class="dci-letter" aria-hidden="true">D</span>
              <h3>Data</h3>
              <p>Carried by the objects. It stays stable and doesn't know about any particular scenario.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="dci-col key">
              <span class="dci-letter" aria-hidden="true">C</span>
              <h3>Context</h3>
              <p>The goal in motion. It exists for the duration of the work and then it is done.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="dci-col">
              <span class="dci-letter" aria-hidden="true">I</span>
              <h3>Interaction</h3>
              <p>The messages, and the roles objects play while the goal is being worked on.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            None of this needs a class hierarchy, and no object has to know ahead of time which
            scenarios it will be useful in. That's what lets the system keep taking on new work
            without redesigning its parts. You can watch a goal go through this cycle in
            <a href="/why#emergence">the sprint loop</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 06 -->
        <section id="love-letter" class="th-sec">
          <SectionHead num="06" title="A Love Letter" />

          <p>
            Abject is a love letter to this idea of messages. It's a love letter to the ultimate
            metasystem, to late binding, and to symbiogenesis, another idea from biology. And it's
            a love letter to the people who kept saying that the space between things is where the
            design lives, all those decades the industry spent drawing hierarchies.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is new. Kay described it in the seventies, Alexander in the seventies
            too, Naur in the eighties, Reenskaug and Coplien in the two thousands. What was missing
            was something that could sit between objects and handle the ambiguity rigid interfaces
            never could. LLMs are good at that. The architecture is older than the model that
            finally made it work.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Abject adds is making the idea concrete for today's software. Every object can
            explain itself. Every object can be discovered by conversation. New bridges get created
            by asking instead of hard-coding, and what lasts is the theory, not the pile of source.
          </p>

          <p class="poster-quote th-quote">
            It's a simple idea but a powerful one. Hard to grasp its scope. Kind of like the Schrödinger equation.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can put it on a t-shirt and spend a lifetime trying to understand it. I'm not sure
            if this is the future of computing architecture. What I do know is that object systems
            scale, the internet proves it, and we can now create objects quickly with LLMs. All you
            have to do is ask.
          </p>

          <p class="aside">
            The long versions, on my blog:
            <a href="https://blog.mempko.com/a-love-letter-to-object-orientation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Love Letter to Object Orientation</a>,
            <a href="https://blog.mempko.com/entering-the-architecture-age/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Entering the Architecture Age</a>,
            <a href="https://blog.mempko.com/an-abject-horror/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">An Abject Horror</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* ── Page hero ── */
  .page-hero {
    padding: calc(66px + 5.5rem) 0 3.5rem;
  }

  .page-hero-title {
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 7.4rem);
    line-height: 0.92;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
  }

  .page-hero-title::after {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 140px;
    height: 6px;
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    background: var(--red);
    clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, calc(100% - 8px) 100%, 0 100%);
  }

  .page-hero-lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 40rem;
    color: var(--bone-2);
  }

  /* ── Reading column plus a side rail ── */
  .theory-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 37rem) minmax(250px, 310px);
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 4rem;
    align-items: start;
    padding-bottom: 5rem;
  }

  .theory-rail {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
    position: sticky;
    top: 92px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  .theory-body { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }

  /* Rail blocks: a mono label on a red rule, then plain ruled rows. */
  .rail-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bone-meta);
    padding-bottom: 0.6rem;
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--red);
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .rail-note p:not(.rail-label) {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--bone-2);
  }

  .toc .rail-label { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .toc-list { list-style: none; }

  .toc-list a {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2.1rem 1fr;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-mid);
    color: var(--bone-2);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: padding-left 0.15s, color 0.15s;
  }

  .toc-num {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--red);
  }

  .toc-list a:hover { padding-left: 0.35rem; color: var(--bone-heading); }

  /* ── Sections ── */
  .th-sec {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 1.5rem;
    border-top: var(--rule-w) solid var(--rule);
    scroll-margin-top: 80px;
  }

  .th-sec:first-child { border-top: none; padding-top: 0.5rem; }

  .th-sec :global(.section-head) { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .th-sec :global(.section-title) { font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); }

  .th-sec p {
    font-size: 1.14rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--bone-2);
    margin-bottom: 1.25em;
  }

  .th-sec p em { color: var(--bone-heading); }

  .th-sec h3 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    margin: 2.4rem 0 0.9rem;
  }

  .th-sec h3::before {
    content: '';
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    background: var(--red);
  }

  /* The one primitive, set large. */
  .primitive {
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1.75rem;
    color: var(--bone-heading);
    box-shadow: var(--print);
  }

  /* ── Quote: a red-ruled block ── */
  .quote {
    position: relative;
    border-left: 5px solid var(--red);
    padding: 3.2rem 0 0.4rem 1.8rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 2.5rem;
  }

  .quote::before {
    content: '\201C';
    position: absolute;
    left: 1.6rem;
    top: -0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 6rem;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--red);
  }

  .th-sec .quote p {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bone-heading);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }

  .th-sec .quote p:first-child {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .quote cite {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-style: normal;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bone-meta);
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }

  .quote cite::before { content: '\25A0'; color: var(--red); margin-right: 0.6rem; font-size: 0.8em; }

  /* ── Thesis lines as poster quotes, sized to the reading column ── */
  .th-sec p.th-quote {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.7vw, 2.05rem);
    line-height: 1.12;
    color: var(--bone-heading);
    margin: 2.75rem 0;
  }

  /* ── The manifesto note: a small paper inset ── */
  .note {
    max-width: 30rem;
    margin: 2.25rem 0 1rem auto;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem 1.3rem;
    border-top: 6px solid var(--red);
    box-shadow: var(--print);
    transform: rotate(-0.6deg);
  }

  .th-sec .note-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--red);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .th-sec .note p:not(.note-label) { margin: 0; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; color: #3f392f; }
  .th-sec .note strong { color: #16130f; }

  .th-sec p.aside {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--bone-meta);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .th-sec p.aside::before {
    content: '\25B6';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--red);
    font-size: 0.7em;
  }

  /* ── DCI: three columns, the letters set huge ── */
  .dci {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.75rem;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 2.5rem;
  }

  .dci-col { border-top: var(--rule-w) solid var(--rule-strong); padding-top: 0.6rem; }
  .dci-col.key { border-top-color: var(--red); }

  .dci-letter {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(5rem, 11vw, 8rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    color: var(--bone-faint);
  }

  .dci-col.key .dci-letter { color: var(--red); }

  .th-sec .dci-col h3 { margin: 0.6rem 0 0.4rem; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .th-sec .dci-col h3::before { display: none; }
  .th-sec .dci-col p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.6; }

  /* ── Responsive ── */
  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .theory-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2.5rem; }
    .theory-rail {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: auto;
      position: static;
      max-width: 37rem;
    }
    .theory-body { grid-column: 1; grid-row: auto; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .page-hero { padding-top: calc(66px + 3rem); padding-bottom: 2.5rem; }
    .th-sec p { font-size: 1.05rem; }
    .dci { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
    .dci-letter { font-size: 4rem; }
    .quote { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
    .th-sec .quote p { font-size: 1.12rem; }
    .note { margin-left: 0; transform: none; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .dci { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.25rem; }
    .dci-col { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 4rem minmax(0, 1fr); column-gap: 1rem; align-items: start; }
    .dci-letter { grid-row: span 2; font-size: 4.2rem; }
    .th-sec .dci-col h3 { margin-top: 0.2rem; }
  }
</style>
